.TH AZKABAN 1 "2026-09" "azkaban" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
azkaban \- a jail for AI CLIs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B azkaban
.RI [ flags ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B azkaban why
.RI [ flags ]
.I path|host|port
.br
.B azkaban rollback
.RB { show | restore }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B azkaban
confines a coding agent (Claude Code, aider and the like) to a single project
directory using bubblewrap and Landlock, so that a misbehaving or hallucinating
tool cannot read your private keys, exfiltrate credentials, or delete a home
directory.
.PP
The primary threat it is arranged around is accidental destruction \(em an agent
that misreads a path and deletes the wrong folder \(em not a determined
attacker. Network isolation is secondary and off by default.
.PP
By default writes to allowlisted directories under
.I $HOME
land on a throwaway overlay: the tool sees them as writable, but the writes
evaporate on exit and cannot destroy real data.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Devices and display
.TP
.B \-\-no\-gpu
Do not bind GPU devices
.RI ( /dev/nvidia* ", " /dev/dri ).
.TP
.B \-\-display
Pass through X11/Wayland,
.IR XAUTHORITY ,
and the Wayland and PulseAudio sockets from
.IR /run/user .
Off by default; the ssh agent, gpg-agent, dbus and any rootless container socket
in there stay hidden.
.SS Filesystem
.TP
.B \-\-persist
Let writes to
.I $HOME
allowlist directories really land on disk, instead of the throwaway overlay.
.TP
.BI \-\-ro " PATH"
Bind one extra path read-only, for this run only. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-rw " PATH"
The same, writable (still overlaid unless
.BR \-\-persist ).
Repeatable, relative to
.IR $HOME ;
.I /
and
.I $HOME
themselves are refused. Un-masks any credential store named.
.TP
.BI \-\-persist\-path " PATH"
Exempt one path from the throwaway overlay: writes to it land on the host while
everything else still evaporates. For the file a tool must keep across runs (a
login token) without
.B \-\-persist
making the whole allowlist destroyable. Repeatable; name the file, not its
directory.
.TP
.B \-\-rollback
Snapshot the writable
.I $HOME
roots either side of the run and let writes land for real, so that destruction
becomes a diff to review rather than a loss. An alternative to the default
overlay, not a layer on it. Review and undo with
.BR "azkaban rollback show|restore" .
.SS Container and unix sockets
.TP
.B \-\-bind\-docker
Bind the Docker socket behind the filtering proxy. Off by default; containerd is
not offered, as it speaks gRPC, which the proxy cannot inspect.
.TP
.B \-\-bind\-podman
The same, for Podman's Docker-compatible REST socket.
.TP
.B \-\-unfiltered\-container\-socket
Bind the socket with no filtering at all. This says how to bind, not which \(em
pair it with
.B \-\-bind\-docker
or
.BR \-\-bind\-podman .
.TP
.BI \-\-unix\-socket " PATH"
Bind one unix socket, and nothing around it \(em for "this tool may reach
Postgres at
.IR /tmp/.s.PGSQL.5432 ""
without granting
.IR /tmp .
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-unix\-socket\-dir " DIR"
The same, for a directory whose socket names are generated at runtime
(PID-suffixed paths). Wider by exactly one directory, which is why both exist.
Repeatable.
.SS Credentials
.TP
.B \-\-ssh\-agent
Forward the agent, plus
.I known_hosts
read-only, so that git push over ssh works. The jail talks to a filtering proxy
in the outer process that forwards only "list keys" and "sign this", refusing
add, remove, lock and extensions. The keys stay on the host either way; the jail
gets a signing oracle, and that oracle still authenticates as you to every host
it can open.
.TP
.B \-\-ssh\-agent\-confirm
As above, and ask on the terminal before every signature. This is
.B "ssh-add -c"
for a jail that cannot reach the host's prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-ssh\-agent\-raw
Bind the real agent socket with no filter \(em the pre-proxy behaviour. Anything
in the jail can then add, remove or lock keys as well as sign with them.
.TP
.BI \-\-credential " PROVIDER"
Let the jail use a host credential without giving it the secret: it talks plain
HTTP to a loopback broker, which attaches the real token and makes the TLS
connection itself.
.B github
is the only provider so far; append
.B " write"
to permit push, which the default read-only policy refuses. Repeatable.
.SS Network
.TP
.B \-\-no\-net
Isolate the network in a new namespace. This breaks internet access.
.TP
.BI \-\-net\-ports " LIST"
Allow outbound TCP only to these ports (comma-separated), enforced by Landlock.
Blocks localhost services and LAN scanning. UDP, and therefore DNS, is
unaffected. Needs the Landlock stage.
.TP
.BI \-\-net\-host " HOST"
Allow outbound traffic only to this host, through a CONNECT proxy in the outer
process. Repeatable;
.B *.example.com
covers subdomains but not the bare domain. TLS is not intercepted: the target is
checked and raw bytes relayed. Sets
.I HTTPS_PROXY
in the jail and narrows
.B \-\-net\-ports
to the proxy, so a client that ignores the variable is refused by the kernel
rather than connecting directly. Needs the Landlock stage.
.SS Resources and environment
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-env
Inherit the whole host environment. The default is to clear it and pass only
.IR HOME ", " PATH ", " TERM ", " LANG
and similar; add more with
.B "env NAME"
lines in
.IR ~/.config/azkaban/config .
.TP
.BI \-\-mem\-max " SIZE"
Cap total memory with a cgroup, e.g.
.BR 8G .
The overlay writes to tmpfs, i.e. RAM, and this is the only thing that bounds
it. Off by default: a cap also disables swap for the jail, so a workload that
would have paged out is killed instead.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-rlimits
Do not cap file size or process count. The default caps them, because the
overlay writes to RAM and a runaway write can OOM the host.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-userns
Permit nested user namespaces, needed by Chrome- and Electron-based tools.
.SS Enforcement and diagnostics
.TP
.B \-\-no\-landlock
Skip the Landlock stage.
.TP
.B \-\-elevate
Let a denied read be approved on the terminal instead of ending the run. A
seccomp supervisor in the outer process traps opens, asks about any path outside
the allowlist, and hands back a read-only descriptor it opened itself \(em so a
grant is bounded by your own permissions and cannot exceed them. Landlock stays
the floor. Writes are never elevated. Off by default, and rate-limited to 10
prompts per second.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the bubblewrap command instead of running it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-guidance
Do not describe the jail to the tool inside it. By default
.I /run/azkaban
holds a read-only
.IR policy.json ,
a README, a Claude Code PostToolUse hook and this binary, so that a confused
agent can run
.B "azkaban why \-\-self"
instead of guessing at an error.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-audit
Do not record this run. Every run is otherwise written as JSONL to
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/azkaban/audit/ :
the resolved policy, the mode flags, every degradation, every docker-filter
decision and the exit code.
.B "audit off"
in the config turns it off for good.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the flag reference.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B azkaban why
Explain what the jail would do with one path, host or port, without starting
one. See
.B "azkaban why \-h"
for its flags.
.TP
.B azkaban rollback
List, review and undo what a
.B \-\-rollback
run changed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/azkaban/config
Per-run defaults:
.BR ro ", " rw ", " persist ", " net ", " "unix-socket" ", " credential ", " env
and
.B audit
lines.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/azkaban/audit/
Append-only JSONL record of every run.
.TP
.I /run/azkaban
Guidance handed to the tool inside the jail.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of the confined
.IR command .
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run an agent confined to the current project:
.PP
.RS
.B azkaban \-\- claude
.RE
.PP
Allow git push, but nothing else on the network:
.PP
.RS
.B azkaban \-\-credential "github write" \-\-net\-host github.com \-\- claude
.RE
.PP
See what would happen to one path, without starting a jail:
.PP
.RS
.B azkaban why ~/.config/azkaban/config
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bwrap (1),
.BR landlock (7),
.BR seccomp (2).
Project documentation:
https://github.com/fabiocicerchia/azkaban
